Because the false rumors have uses that are very hard to get.

> "Ever heard hissing outside?  I *knew* you hadn't!"
>
>  I guess it says someting about a cockatrice, but I cannot
>  understand what the 'outside' means, and why '*knew*' is
>  a past tense and is emphasized.

The false part: Footrice do not appear out of the dungeon, but
the phase of the moon effects their attack effectiveness.  Only
partially false since it gives context.

The language part: Discussions of contradictions and
conundrums and confusion often includes "I knew it!" in
English.  The language suggests that there is an easy meaning
on the surface (no footrice outside of the dungeon) and also
a hidden meaning (I invite other useful interpretations than the
phase of the moon).

> "Liquor sellers do not drink; they hate to see you twice."
>
>  I don't see why the liquor sellers see you twice if they
>  drink. Someone say that the first you is you the drunk
>  and the second you is the liqor seller the drunk. But
>  I'm not sure.

The false part - Shopkeepers get a wand in their inventory not
a potion.  Since shopkeepers never pick up their own inventory
of course they don't drink their own merchandise.

The language part - This discusses both vision and travel.
If you visit a shop, pay and come back they do not hate to
see you twice.  If you teleport out of a shop to steal they hate
you whether you come back or not.  If you are invisible they
hate to NOT see you and it doesn't matter how many times.
And if you end up in combat whileyou have displacement
"Izchak is not fooled by your displaced image" because a
shopkeeper is always fixed in position while fighting so they
have a better chance than most monsters of locking in on
your displaced image because you can't move as easily either.

There may also be an issue of "seeing" meaning "line of sight"
and shopkeepers wanting to avoid line of sight wand zaps but
they are very limited in movement ...

> "You can't get by the snake."
>
>  Why does it say 'the snake', not 'a snake'?

It is a direct quote from a famous old game as others have
mentioned.  In Advent the snake blocks your path.

> I guess it says
>  more than that "You can't pass in front of a snake without
>  any trouble."

The false part - In Nethack you can usually go around snakes.
They are not fast and sometimes they go into hiding.  It is not
snakes that stick to you.

The language part - There are monsters in Nethack that block
your path.  They are not snakes.  There are many ways to get
past these monsters.  There are monsters in Nethack that
hide.  The methods that find hidden snakes also find these types
of monsters.  Consider telepathy - I suspect that no hiding monsters
are mindless.



